{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794604979,"nonce":"GDLmtTs3Jy2HFDoPAIPWI/RmnVA=","message":"38f3277: every generated HashLink primitive is named after its library: the Native classes use @:hlNative(\"\u003clibrary\u003e\", \"\u003clibrary\u003e_\"), as Haxe's standard library does (@:hlNative(\"std\", \"atomic_\")), and every resolver is hlp_\u003clibrary\u003e_..., including the library-wide future_/stream_/signal_/outbox_ primitives. A phone links all libraries and the runtime into one program, where future_state collided with Ash's runtime export hlp_future_state and two hxn libraries would collide with each other (issue 0d4fa65). d4d6d27 fixes the CLI's adapter test, which 38f3277 left expecting the old names. 7f2da62 records the naming in docs/conventions/boundary.md.\n\ne6ccd03 closes the open point about a library whose name is not a good class name: an interface, data type or class (the library's default one included) named like a type of Haxe's top-level package (Math, String, Map, Date, Sys, ... from the installed std and StdTypes) is refused, since the generated classes share that package and the generated code uses those types. A library named math puts its functions on class = \"Name\". Left open from that point: a doc comment for a class that holds only functions.","files":null}]}